  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAn upper low over the Pacific NW finally moves inland to over the \nnorthern Rockies Monday shifting the upper high east to over the \ncentral and southern Great Plains. The position of these two upper \nlevel features will help position the monsoon plume from southwest \nto northeast NM. Scattered to numerous showers and storms will favor \nthe western and central mountains with an overall storm motion to \nthe northeast at 10 to 20 mph trying to move in nearby lower \nelevations later in the late afternoon and early evening hours \nbefore tapering off around sunset. The upper low exits northeast to \nthe Dakotas and central Canada Tuesday flattening the upper high \nfrom eastern NM to central and southern Appalachians and \nstrengthening to 597 dam over the mid Mississippi, Ohio and \nTennessee River Valleys. The monsoon plume and thus better shower \nand storm coverage will remain across western and central NM due to \nthis part of the state remaining on the western flank of the upper \nhigh circulation. \n\nThe 597 dam 500 mb upper high looks to pivot back west to over New \nMexico as early as Wednesday. This will help disrupt the monsoon \nplume Wednesday and Thursday with recycled moisture helping to keep \ndiurnal shower and storm chances across the higher terrain. A \nbackdoor front could enter northeast and far east central NM \nThursday due to a slightly deeper shortwave over the central U.S. \naccording to the GFS and AI-GFS resulting in a moisture boost and \nbetter coverage of showers and storms across this part of the state. \nHowever, the ECMWF and cluster guidance has the shortwave over the \ncentral U.S. more shallow and keeps the 594 to 595 dam upper high \nmore overhead with no hint of a backdoor front. The upper high over \nthe desert SW looks to weaken a little more to 592 to 593 dam at 500 \nmb on Friday. However, guidance differs on the amount of available \nmoisture with the GFS having PWATS around 0.5 to 0.9 from north to \nsouth while the ECMWF has PWATS around 1 to 1.2 inches areawide. \nCluster analysis is split. Right now it looks like scattered showers \nand storms will favor the western and central mountains and nearby \nlower elevations during the afternoon and early evening hours. Highs \nnext week will be in the upper 80s to mid 90s across lower \nelevations of western and central NM and mid to upper 90s across \neastern NM which is slightly above average for early to mid \nSeptember.",
